<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Musunuri Nayakas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1336–1368</span> · Also known as Musunuri Nayaks</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Musunuri <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nayaka">Nayakas</a> were Telugu chiefs who led the revolt that drove the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a> out of the Kakatiya lands of Andhra and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telangana">Telangana</a> in the 1330s. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prolaya-nayaka">Prolaya Nayaka</a> and his cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kapaya-nayaka">Kapaya Nayaka</a> they ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warangal">Warangal</a>, but wars with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahmani-sultanate">Bahmani Sultanate</a> cost them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golconda-fort">Golconda</a>, and Kapaya Nayaka was killed by the Recherla chiefs around 1368.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/musunuri-nayakas.jpg" alt="Musunuri Nayakas" width="320" height="225" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vinay332211 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lepakshi...jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
